---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const updated = 'May 1, 2026';
---

<!--
  Application home page for the OAuth verification submission to Google.
  This URL ("https://vhsgarage.com/recorder") is what we list as the
  "Application home page" in the Google Cloud OAuth consent screen.

  Two verification rejection reasons we're addressing here:
    1. "Your home page does not explain the purpose of your app."
       → Sections below spell out exactly what the app does.
    2. "App name 'VHS Garage' configured for your OAuth consent screen
        does not match the app name on your home page."
       → The H1 below is the literal string "VHS Garage" so reviewers
         see a 1:1 name match.

  Reviewer checklist this page is built around:
    • App name visible at the top (matches OAuth consent screen)
    • One-sentence purpose
    • Plain-English description
    • Explicit list of OAuth scopes + why each is needed
    • What the app does NOT access
    • Where data lives
    • Links to Privacy + Terms
    • Visible link to start using the app
-->

<Base
  title="VHS Garage Recorder"
  description="A free browser-based VHS digitizer that records your tapes and uploads them to your own YouTube channel."
>
  <div class="min-h-screen px-6 py-12 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
    <a href="/" class="text-white/30 text-xs hover:text-white/50 transition-colors">← vhsgarage.com</a>

    <!-- App name MUST match the OAuth consent screen's "App name" field
         exactly — currently "VHS Garage". Don't change without also
         updating the consent screen. -->
    <h1 class="text-4xl mt-8 mb-2">VHS Garage</h1>
    <p class="text-white/50 text-xs mb-2">A free browser-based VHS digitizer.</p>
    <p class="text-white/40 text-xs mb-10">Last updated: {updated}</p>

    <div class="space-y-8 text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl text-white mb-3">What this app does</h2>
        <p class="mb-3">
          VHS Garage is a free, browser-based tool that helps you digitize old VHS
          tapes and (optionally) publish them to your own YouTube channel. You plug
          a USB capture card into your computer, connect your VCR to the capture
          card, and use this app to record the playback to a video file.
        </p>
        <p>
          Once a clip is recorded you can fill out title, description, year,
          distributor, tape format, and other metadata, optionally let an AI
          rewrite the copy from a photo of the VHS sleeve, pick a thumbnail
          frame, and upload the final video to your own YouTube channel.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl text-white mb-3">Get started</h2>
        <p class="mb-4">
          Open the app in Chrome (the only browser that supports the local-file
          and capture APIs we rely on). Pick a save folder on your computer,
          choose your capture card and microphone, and hit Record.
        </p>
        <a href="/capture" class="inline-block px-4 py-2 border border-[#dc2626] text-[#dc2626] hover:bg-[#dc2626]/10 text-sm uppercase tracking-wider transition-colors">
          Open the recorder →
        </a>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl text-white mb-3">YouTube permissions we request</h2>
        <p class="mb-4">
          When you choose to publish a recorded clip to YouTube, the app uses
          Google OAuth so the upload goes to <strong class="text-white">your own
          YouTube channel</strong>, not ours. We request the minimum scopes
          required to do that.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-3">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">youtube.upload</strong>
            <span class="block text-white/60 text-sm">
              To upload the video file you recorded to your YouTube channel,
              along with its title, description, tags, and chosen thumbnail.
              We never upload anything you didn't explicitly click "Upload" on.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">youtube.readonly</strong>
            <span class="block text-white/60 text-sm">
              To display your channel name and handle in the publish panel so
              you can confirm which channel you're about to upload to before
              clicking Upload. Used purely to label the UI; we do not read,
              list, or store any of your existing videos, playlists,
              subscriptions, or watch history.
            </span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl text-white mb-3">What we do not access</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
          <li>Your existing YouTube videos, playlists, or watch history</li>
          <li>Your subscriptions, comments, or community posts</li>
          <li>Your YouTube analytics or revenue data</li>
          <li>Your Gmail, Google Drive, Calendar, Contacts, or any other Google service</li>
          <li>Any video you record that you don't explicitly click Upload on</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl text-white mb-3">Where your data lives</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">Your recordings</strong> are saved
            directly to a folder on your own computer that you choose. They
            never touch our servers.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">Your clip metadata</strong> (titles,
            descriptions, year, etc.) is stored in your browser's local
            storage and as plain-text JSON files alongside the videos in your
            chosen folder.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">Your YouTube OAuth token</strong> is
            stored in your browser only. We do not have access to it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">AI rewrites</strong> are performed by
            sending the sleeve photo and clip metadata to Google's Gemini API
            for that single request. We do not store the photos or the
            generated text on any server.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl text-white mb-3">Privacy &amp; Terms</h2>
        <p class="mb-2">
          For the full details on data handling and your rights, see our
          <a href="/privacy" class="text-[#dc2626] hover:underline">Privacy Policy</a>
          and
          <a href="/terms" class="text-[#dc2626] hover:underline">Terms of Use</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl text-white mb-3">Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Questions, bug reports, or YouTube verification follow-ups:
          <a href="mailto:hello@vhsgarage.com" class="text-[#dc2626] hover:underline">hello@vhsgarage.com</a>
        </p>
      </section>

    </div>

    <p class="text-white/30 text-xs mt-16">&copy; 2026 VHS Garage</p>
  </div>
</Base>
